# Documentation lifecycle: shekyl-core's part

Estate rule [`95-documentation-lifecycle`](95-documentation-lifecycle.mdc) holds in every Shekyl repository.
This rule holds the part of it that only shekyl-core has: its front door
and archive checklist, the spec names a class deletion never touches,
the FOLLOWUPS `Owner:` gate, the `changelog.d` mechanism and the
document gates.

## Front door and archive checklist

`docs/README.md` is the front door.

Cite from [`91-documentation-after-plans.mdc`](91-documentation-after-plans.mdc)
and [`194-tracking-index.mdc`](194-tracking-index.mdc). Archive eligibility
and the `git mv` checklist live in
[`docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md`](../../docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md)
§8; this rule does not restate that checklist.

## Protected specification names

Claim-era staking specs (confidential / lock-tier / `txin_stake_claim*`)
are deleted, not stubbed. Archival staking SoTs (`V3_STAKER_ARCHIVAL.md`,
`PHASE_2B_FSM_RETOOL.md`, `PRINCIPAL_STAKE_LIFECYCLE.md`,
`REWARD_EMISSION_*`, `ARCHIVAL_*.md`, `EMISSION_CLAIM_BUILDER.md`) are
never deleted as a side-effect of that class.

## The FOLLOWUPS `Owner:` cell

`Owner:` names what the row **resolves to**, and it is gated
(`scripts/ci/check_followups_owners.py`): a live `docs/design/` document,
or a registered identifier family (`RD-Q4`, `SI-10`). A PR number may be
cited beside it but is not an owner on its own — PRs merge and close, and
a row whose only owner is a merged PR is a row nobody owns. Six rows once
named "the E2 lane" as owner while no such lane existed in any form; that
is the shape the cell makes visible (rule 22). Rows predating the cell are
grandfathered by exact heading in a list that only burns down.

## CHANGELOG mechanics and document gates

A pull request writes its entry as a new file under `docs/changelog.d/`,
named after its branch. It never edits `docs/CHANGELOG.md`. Only the
release cut (`docs/RELEASING.md` step 1) moves entries into the
CHANGELOG. An edit at the top of `[Unreleased]` conflicts with every
other open pull request that makes one, and each conflict costs a merge
of `dev` and a full CI run.

CI (`.github/workflows/docs-gates.yml`) holds the banners, FOLLOWUPS
`Target:` tokens, forbidden fossils in always-applied rules, index §2
prefix uniqueness, and the claim-era basename refusal. A closed plan
left in `docs/design/` with a DRAFT banner, or a new
`CONFIDENTIAL_STAKING.md`, fails the run.

